  "verbalization": "Oxybuprocaine, Proparacaine, and Procaine all share a common structural feature of being benzoate esters, which is a key factor in their pharmacological activity. This structural similarity allows them to bind to the same target, Scn10a, through a shared mechanism of interaction. The evidence suggests that Oxybuprocaine, through its resemblance to Proparacaine and Procaine, may also bind to Scn10a, despite the lower similarity scores in some paths. The recurring role of benzoate esters and the shared binding to Scn10a indicates a potential mechanism of action that is consistent across these compounds. The presence of anesthetic agents and organooxygen compounds in the lowest common ancestors further supports the idea that these compounds interact with the nervous system, possibly through a common pathway involving ion channel modulation. The integration of these findings suggests that Oxybuprocaine may act on Scn10a via a mechanism that is structurally and functionally related to other benzoate esters, highlighting a broader pharmacological profile that warrants further investigation.",
